      "id": "2072253537040605305",
      "text": "I installed an iOS app on my iPhone last night without opening the App Store. From a website. The server I used couldn't even see my Apple ID.\n\nIt's called AssppWeb. It's a self-hostable web tool that authenticates with your Apple ID, acquires app licenses through Apple's official APIs, and installs the IPAs directly to your iPhone.\n\nThe architecture is what makes it different from every other sideloading setup out there.\n\nEvery other approach trusts the server. You log in, the server holds your credentials, you hope they don't get logged. AssppWeb runs zero-trust. All Apple API traffic happens inside your browser via libcurl.js compiled to WebAssembly, encrypted with Mbed TLS 1.3. The backend is a blind TCP relay running the Wisp protocol over WebSocket. It cannot decrypt a single byte of what passes through it.\n\n→ Apple ID authentication runs entirely in your browser, never in plaintext on the server\n→ Backend acts as a blind TCP relay, zero credential exposure\n→ Acquires licenses and downloads IPAs from Apple's public CDN\n→ Installs to iPhone via itms-services:// install links\n→ One-click deploy to Cloudflare Workers + Containers or Railway\n→ Self-host on Docker in two commands\n→ Optional password gate on your instance\n\n2.4K stars. MIT License. 100% Opensource.\n\nRepo: https://t.co/byj3mxzr2w",
